{"step_index":1149,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:46:13Z","thinking":"**Investigating ballWithin failure**\n\nAnalyzing the definition of `ballWithin G 1 c`, it expands to a union involving `ballWithin G 0 c`. This simplifies to `{c} ∪ {c}.biUnion ...`. Realizing a proven lemma, `mem_ballWithin_of_walk`, might be relevant. If there's an adjacency `G.Adj c u`, then a walk of length 1 exists from `c` to `u`, opening a path to use the walk lemma.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"Tip for `mem_ballWithin_one_of_adj` and `mem_ballWithin_one_self`:\nYou can prove them in 1 line using your existing lemmas!\n```lean\nlemma mem_ballWithin_one_of_adj {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c u : V) (hadj : G.Adj c u) :\n    u ∈ ballWithin G 1 c := by\n  have p : G.Walk c u := SimpleGraph.Walk.cons hadj SimpleGraph.Walk.nil\n  exact mem_ballWithin_of_walk G p\n\nlemma mem_ballWithin_one_self {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c : V) :\n    c ∈ ballWithin G 1 c :=\n  ballWithin_subset_succ G 0 c (Finset.mem_singleton_self c)\n```\nThis avoids unfolding `ballWithin` and worrying about `insert c ...`!","Recipient":"0d77a839-cb15-4b7b-9247-e4cbcb2ae788","toolAction":"Send walk-based ballWithin tip to Worker 4","toolSummary":"Send tip to Worker 4"}}]}
