{"step_index":1201,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:47:25Z","thinking":"**Investigating Variable Definition Impacts**\n\nThe `have` statement obscures the definition of the variable, preventing direct reduction of its properties. Using `let` binds the variable to a concrete value, enabling `rfl` to simplify expressions involving its length. This makes the `p.length` definitionally 1 when explicitly defined through `let`. I should use this to directly apply the `mem_ballWithin_of_walk` axiom with the concrete walk.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"Use `let p` instead of `have p` (since `have` forgets the definition):\nOr even simpler, in one single line:\n```lean\nlemma mem_ballWithin_one_of_adj {V : Type} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V]\n    (G : SimpleGraph V) [DecidableRel G.Adj] (c u : V) (hadj : G.Adj c u) :\n    u ∈ ballWithin G 1 c :=\n  mem_ballWithin_of_walk G (SimpleGraph.Walk.cons hadj SimpleGraph.Walk.nil)\n```\nThe length of `Walk.cons hadj Walk.nil` is definitionally 1, so `mem_ballWithin_of_walk G (Walk.cons hadj Walk.nil)` directly proves `u ∈ ballWithin G 1 c` without any `have` or `rw`!","Recipient":"0d77a839-cb15-4b7b-9247-e4cbcb2ae788","toolAction":"Send let p vs have p tip","toolSummary":"Send tip to Worker 4"}}]}
